# The life a started thread owes its `wait`, held as a handle of C so that the
# language's own count of lives tells a thread never waited for: a block of
# one byte from `stdlib.h`'s `malloc`, which `acquires free`, given back by
# `free`, which `consumes` it. A handle nobody gave back stops the program
# when `main` returns, saying how many and where each was taken (spec § 13).
# The byte is never read or written; only its life counts.

extern "stdlib.h"
    # The block's address, the one thing the package holds of it.
    record Life tag void
    # A block of `size` bytes, whose life the program then owes `free`.
    function malloc(size: u64) -> Life acquires free
    # Gives the block back, which ends its life.
    function free(p: Life consumes)

# A new life, which the program then owes `end`.
function begin() -> Life
    return malloc(1)

# Ends `life`, which may not be read again.
function end(@life: Life)
    free(life)
